Executive Order 330-B.wikitext:
{{Potus-eo
 | eo       = 330
 | title    = Executive Order 330-B
 | previous = Executive Order 330-A
 | next     = Executive Order 331
 | section  = Amendment of Executive Order of March 26, 1901, Establishing Limits of&nbsp;Punishment for&nbsp;Enlisted&nbsp;Men of&nbsp;the&nbsp;Army
 | defaultsort = Executive Order 00330-B
 | year     = 1905
 | month    = 06
 | day      = 12
 | cite     = ''Published'': [http://books.google.com/books?id=qJMYAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA51 General Orders, No. 96], War Dept., June 19, 1905&nbsp;&nbsp;
 | notes    = See the '''''[[#Notes|Notes]]''''' section for a list of Executive Orders affected by or related to the issuance of this Executive Order.
}}
The Executive order, dated March 26, 1901, establishing limits of punishment for enlisted men of the Army, under an [[Page:United States Statutes at Large Volume 26.djvu/545#Chap. 998.|Act of Congress approved September 27, 1890]], and which was published in General Orders, No. 42, Headquarters of the Army, Adjutant General's Office, March 26, 1901, is amended so as to prescribe as follows:
__NOTOC__

{{c/s}}
==={{section|Art-I|Article I}}.===
{{c/e}}

:In all cases of desertion the sentence may include dishonorable discharge and forfeiture of pay and allowances.

:Subject to the modifications authorized in [[#Art-I-Sec-3|section&nbsp;3]] of this article, the limit of the term of confinement (at hard labor) for desertion shall be as follows:

{{sc|{{section|Art-I-Sec-1|Section&nbsp;1}}.}} In case of surrender—
{| style="margin: 0em 0em 0em 2.0em;"
|-valign="top"
| (''a'') 
| When the deserter surrenders himself after an absence of not more than thirty days, one year.
|-valign="top"
| (''b'') 
| When the surrender is made after an absence of more than thirty days, eighteen months.
|}

{{sc|{{section|Art-I-Sec-2|Sec.&nbsp;2}}.}} In case of apprehension—
{| style="margin: 0em 0em 0em 2.0em;"
|-valign="top"
| (''a'') 
| When at the time of desertion the deserter shall not have been more than six months in the service, eighteen months.
|-valign="top"
| (''b'') 
| When he shall have been more than six months in the service, two and one-half years.
|}

{{sc|{{section|Art-I-Sec-3|Sec.&nbsp;3}}.}} The foregoing limitations are subject to modification under the following conditions:
{| style="margin: 0em 0em 0em 2.0em;"
|-valign="top"
| (''a'') 
| The punishment of a deserter may be increased by one year of confinement at hard labor in consideration of each previous conviction of desertion.
|-valign="top"
| (''b'') 
| The punishment for desertion when joined in by two or more soldiers in the execution of a conspiracy, or for desertion in the presence of an outbreak of Indians or of any unlawful assemblage which the troops may be opposing, shall not exceed dishonorable discharge, forfeiture of all pay and allowances, and confinement at hard labor for five years.
|}


{{c/s}}
==={{section|Art-II|Article II}}.===
